{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>For the Name of HaShem I call.</b> The intention is that the matter toward which he aims in his utterances is the Name of HaShem, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Zohar III 98a): the entire Torah is nothing but the names of the Holy One, blessed be He. And concerning this it says ״יַעֲרֹף כַּמָּטָר לִקְחִי״ — which is the Torah, which is His Name, blessed be He. And the meaning of ״אֶקְרָא״ is from the root of יְקָר (preciousness/honor)." "<b>Further,</b> he hinted that even though he hung the matter of Torah study on dew and rain, the intention in study should not be for this, but rather to read in the book of the Torah of HaShem. And this is what he says — ״כִּי״, meaning: the reason shall be ״שֵׁם ה׳ אֶקְרָא״, meaning, to read in His Torah, which is His Name, blessed be He, in order to grant greatness to our Elohim. And this is what is called Torah for its own sake — to magnify and glorify the Name of His glory. And his saying ״אֶקְרָא״ is what each one of them will do. Or, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Tikkunei Zohar, Tikkun 69, p. 112b), that the souls of all who engage in Torah are sparks of Moses — and not the wilderness generation alone, but to the end of all generations, everyone who engages in Torah is bound up with Moses. Therefore he attributed the Torah engagement of Israel to himself and said ״אֶקְרָא״. Understand this:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that ״כִּי שֵׁם ה׳״ etc. is giving the reason for what is said immediately afterward, namely ״הָבוּ גֹדֶל לֵאלֹהֵינוּ״. And it is along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Shokher Tov on Psalms 22:4), concerning the verse (Psalms 22:4) ״יוֹשֵׁב תְּהִלּוֹת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that HaShem did not sit upon the throne of praises until the heavenly hosts said ״בָּרוּךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״. And He had no gratification when they called Him Elohim of heaven and earth, or Elohim of the angels. Therefore His good word comes here and says: grant greatness and exaltation to this matter — that He does not desire to be called anything but ״our Elohim,״ which is what He says: ״לֵאלֹהֵינוּ״. For in the very act of reading in the Torah, which is the Name of HaShem — which is what He says, ״כִּי שֵׁם ה׳ אֶקְרָא״ — through this, when the celestial beings praise and say ״בָּרוּךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, this praise contains greatness and exaltation; which is not so when Israel does not engage in Torah and the angels say ״בָּרוּךְ ה׳ אֱלֹהֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — this praise is greatly diminished, falling lower and lower, and the matter is undermined, for they are, as it were, contradicting — perish the thought — when they do not engage in His Torah, that in its very midst He attributes His divinity to us. And our Sages, of blessed memory, darshened many derashot (Sifrei), like a hammer, etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:09.263905499Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}